<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byzantine commonwealth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Byzantine commonwealth is a term used by historians for the community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian peoples and states culturally shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. These societies adopted Byzantine religion, liturgy, law and artistic forms. The concept describes how the cultural reach of the Byzantine Empire extended far beyond its political borders and outlasted it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term was coined by the 20th-century historian Dimitri Obolensky, whose study The Byzantine Commonwealth is the most important treatment of the idea; in his book Six Byzantine Portraits he examined the lives and works of six people who appear in it. Obolensky described the commonwealth as the international community within the sphere of authority of the Byzantine emperor, bound by the same profession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a> and accepting the principles of Romano-Byzantine law.</p><p>The area it describes is that where Byzantine liturgical and cultural tradition was spread in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by the empire and its missionaries. It covers approximately the present-day countries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, a region also called the Orthodox Civilization or the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Other scholars dispute the idea of Byzantine cultural and ideological supremacy behind the model. They argue that documented cultural exchange was complex and many-sided, and did not fit a picture in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was the superior core while the peoples of the periphery accepted a marginal place and merely imitated it. Anthony Kaldellis holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in general had no ecumenical outlook and did not think of a pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> commonwealth, an attitude he describes as Roman chauvinism.</p><p>The historian Christian Raffensperger proposed recasting the commonwealth as the Byzantine ideal, a conception of imperial hierarchy that rulers kept even in the empire's later period, when its reach and sway had diminished. The Bulgarian tsars, though often powerful rivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, framed their authority in Orthodox Roman ideology and artistic style, calling themselves emperors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as well as tsars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. The same applied to the Serbian Empire and even to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, whose sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> styled himself Qayser-i Rûm.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Byzantine commonwealth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Orthodox Christianity that the Byzantine state patronised and defined became the faith of Russia, Serbia, Bulgaria, Romania, Georgia and the Greek world, a “<b>Byzantine commonwealth</b>” that extended the empire's cultural reach far beyond its political frontiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
